            <ArticleTitle>Two cases of Kawasaki disease presented with acute febrile jaundice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-86</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.24953/turkjped.2017.01.015</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">1684</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Kawasaki disease is an acute, systemic vasculitis of unknown etiology. Although gastrointestinal involvement does not belong to the classic diagnostic criteria; diarrhea, abdominal pain, hepatic dysfunction, hydrops of gallbladder, and acute febrile cholestatic jaundice are reported in patients with Kawasaki disease. We describe here two cases presented with fever, and acute jaundice as initial features of Kawasaki disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
